 previously applied a Schur test approach to show that such a constant \\(C\'\\) can be found.\n\n3.\n\nInstructions:\n1.  Analyze the tool\'s required parameters from its metadata.\n2.  Construct valid Python code that addresses the sub-goal using the provided context and data.\n3.  The command must include at least one call to `tool.execute()`.\n4.  Each `tool.execute()` call must be assigned to a variable named **`execution`**.\n5.  Please give the exact numbers and parameters should be used in the `tool.execute()` call.\n\nOutput Format:\nPresent your response in the following structured format. Do not include any extra text or explanations.\n\nGenerated Command:\n```python\n<command>\n```\n\nExample1:\nGenerated Command:\n```python\nexecution = tool.execute(query="Summarize the following porblom:"Isaac has 100 toys, masa gets ...., how much are their together?")\n```\n\nExample2:\nGenerated Command:\n```python\nexecution = tool.execute(query=["Methanol", "function of hyperbola", "Fermat\'s Last Theorem"])\n```\n', 'gen_ai.response.id': '324cab54-6363-456d-910f-bf62a7613dfb', 'gen_ai.response.model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'gen_ai.openai.system_fingerprint': 'a26a7955944dc5c60445bff77fac9c8e', 'gen_ai.usage.total_tokens': 1061, 'gen_ai.usage.prompt_tokens': 911, 'gen_ai.usage.completion_tokens': 150, 'gen_ai.completion.0.finish_reason': 'stop', 'gen_ai.completion.0.role': 'assistant', 'gen_ai.completion.0.content': 'Generated Command:\n```python\nexecution = tool.execute(query="Consider an infinite sequence of smooth, compactly supported functions {f_n}_{n ≥ 1} on R such that each f_n is orthogonal to C other functions in the sequence, where C is an absolute constant. Determine if there exists an absolute constant C\' such that the following inequality holds: ||sum_{n ≥ 1} f_n||_{L^2(R)} ≤ C\' (sum_{n ≥ 1} ||f_n||_{L^2(R)}^2)^{1/2}. Provide a detailed step-by-step proof or verification using functional analysis techniques, specifically focusing on verifying the result obtained from our initial approach using the Schur test.")\n```'}, 'events': [], 'links': [], 'resource': {'attributes': {'service.name': 'agentops', 'agentops.project.id': 'dummy', 'imported_libraries': ['string', 'sympy', 'uuid', 'json', 'asyncio']}, 'schema_url': ''}}, {'name': 'openai.chat.completion', 'context': {'trace_id': '0x8d00eea93747c0b0e0e486345c632bdd', 'span_id': '0x9dd8e49dafd872f9', 'trace_state': '[]'}, 'kind': 'SpanKind.CLIENT', 'parent_id': None, 'start_time': '2026-08-25T15:18:00.721345Z', 'end_time': '2026-08-25T15:18:18.639668Z', 'status': {'status_code': 'OK'}, 'attributes': {'gen_ai.request.type': 'chat', 'gen_ai.system': 'OpenAI', 'gen_ai.request.model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'gen_ai.request.max_tokens': 4000, 'gen_ai.request.temperature': 0, 'gen_ai.request.top_p': 0.99, 'gen_ai.request.streaming': False, 'gen_ai.prompt.0.role': 'system', 'gen_ai.prompt.0.content': 'You are a helpful assistant.', 'gen_ai.prompt.1.role': 'user', 'gen_ai.prompt.1.content': "Consider an infinite sequence of smooth, compactly supported functions {f_n}_{n ≥ 1} on R such that each f_n is orthogonal to C other functions in the sequence, where C is an absolute constant. Determine if there exists an absolute constant C' such that the following inequality holds: ||sum_{n ≥ 1} f_n||_{L^2(R)} ≤ C' (sum_{n ≥ 1} ||f_n||_{L^2(R)}^2)^{1/2}. Provide a detailed step-by-step proof or verification using functional analysis techniques, specifically focusing on verifying the result obtained from our initial approach using the Schur test.", 'gen_ai.response.id': '7e5f0293-8223-47be-94bc-dfc0238ecaba', 'gen_ai.response.model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'gen_ai.openai.system_fingerprint': 'a26a7955944dc5c60445bff77fac9c8e', 'gen_ai.usage.total_tokens': 2878, 'gen_ai.usage.prompt_tokens': 146, 'gen_ai.usage.completion_tokens': 2732, 'gen_ai.completion.0.finish_reason': 'stop', 'gen_ai.completion.0.role': 'assistant', 'gen_ai.completion.0.content': 'We are given an infinite sequence of smooth, compactly supported functions \\(\\{f_n\\}_{n \\ge 1}\\) on \\(\\mathbb{R}\\), with the property that each \\(f_n\\) is orthogonal to all but at most \\(C\\) other functions in the sequence, where \\(C\\) is an absolute constant.  \nWe want to determine whether there exists an absolute constant \\(C\'\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\left\\| \\sum_{n \\ge 1} f_n \\right\\|_{L^2(\\mathbb{R})} \\le C\' \\left( \\sum_{n \\ge 1} \\|f_n\\|_{L^2(\\mathbb{R})}^2 \\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Reformulating the problem\n\nLet \\(H = L^2(\\mathbb{R})\\). Define the operator \\(T: \\ell^2 \\to H\\) by  \n\n\\[\nT(a) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n f_n,\n\\]\nwhere \\(a = (a_n) \\in \\ell^2\\).  \nWe want to show that \\(T\\) is bounded, i.e., \\(\\|T\\|_{\\text{op}} \\le C\'\\) for some absolute constant \\(C\'\\).\n\nThe norm of \\(T\\) is given by  \n\n\\[\n\\|T\\|_{\\text{op}}^2 = \\sup_{\\|a\\|_{\\ell^2} \\le 1} \\left\\| \\sum_{n} a_n f_n \\right\\|_H^2.\n\\]\n\nExpanding the square:\n\n\\[\n\\left\\| \\sum_n a_n f_n \\right\\|_H^2 \n= \\sum_{n,m} a_n \\overline{a_m} \\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Using the orthogonality structure\n\nWe know each \\(f_n\\) is orthogonal to all but at most \\(C\\) other functions. That means for each fixed \\(n\\), the set  \n\\[\n\\{ m : \\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle \\neq 0 \\}\n\\]  \nhas size at most \\(C+1\\) (including \\(n\\) itself).  \nThus the Gram matrix \\(G = (\\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle)\\) has at most \\(C+1\\) nonzero entries per row and per column.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Applying the Schur test\n\nWe want to bound the quadratic form  \n\\[\n\\sum_{n,m} a_n \\overline{a_m} G_{n,m}.\n\\]\n\nBy Cauchy–Schwarz:\n\n\\[\n\\left| \\sum_{n,m} a_n \\overline{a_m} G_{n,m} \\right|\n\\le \\sum_{n,m} |a_n| |a_m| |G_{n,m}|.\n\\]\n\nWe can apply the **Schur test** for nonnegative matrices. Let \\(M_{n,m} = |G_{n,m}|\\).  \nWe need to find positive weights \\(w_n > 0\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_m M_{n,m} w_m \\le \\lambda w_n \\quad \\text{for all } n,\n\\]\nand  \n\\[\n\\sum_n M_{n,m} w_n \\le \\lambda w_m \\quad \\text{for all } m.\n\\]\n\nTake \\(w_n = 1\\) for all \\(n\\). Then:\n\n- For each \\(n\\),  \n\\[\n\\sum_m M_{n,m} = \\sum_{m: \\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle \\neq 0} |\\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle|.\n\\]  \nBy Cauchy–Schwarz,  \n\\[\n|\\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle| \\le \\|f_n\\| \\|f_m\\|.\n\\]  \nBut we don’t have uniform bounds on \\(\\|f_n\\|\\) — they can be arbitrary. So we need a different approach.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Using the structure more carefully\n\nWe can write  \n\\[\n\\left\\| \\sum_n a_n f_n \\right\\|^2 \n= \\sum_n |a_n|^2 \\|f_n\\|^2 + \\sum_{n \\neq m} a_n \\overline{a_m} \\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe cross terms only occur when \\(\\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle \\neq 0\\), and for each \\(n\\) there are at most \\(C\\) such \\(m \\neq n\\).\n\nThus:\n\n\\[\n\\left| \\sum_{n \\neq m} a_n \\overline{a_m} \\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle \\right|\n\\le \\sum_{n} \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} |a_n| |a_m| |\\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle|,\n\\]\nwhere \\(|S_n| \\le C\\).\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Applying Cauchy–Schwarz to the cross terms\n\nFor each \\(n\\),  \n\\[\n\\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} |a_m| |\\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle|\n\\le \\left( \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} |a_m|^2 \\right)^{1/2}\n\\left( \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} |\\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle|^2 \\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\n\nNow,  \n\\[\n\\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} |\\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle|^2\n\\le \\|f_n\\|^2 \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} \\|f_m\\|^2\n\\]\nby Cauchy–Schwarz on each inner product.\n\nSo  \n\\[\n\\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} |a_m| |\\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle|\n\\le \\|f_n\\| \\left( \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} |a_m|^2 \\right)^{1/2}\n\\left( \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} \\|f_m\\|^2 \\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Step 6: Summing over \\(n\\)\n\nWe have  \n\\[\n\\sum_{n} \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} |a_n| |a_m| |\\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle|\n\\le \\sum_n |a_n| \\|f_n\\| \\left( \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} |a_m|^2 \\right)^{1/2}\n\\left( \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} \\|f_m\\|^2 \\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\n\nApply Cauchy–Schwarz again:\n\n\\[\n\\le \\left( \\sum_n |a_n|^2 \\|f_n\\|^2 \\right)^{1/2}\n\\left( \\sum_n \\left( \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} |a_m|^2 \\right)\n\\left( \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} \\|f_m\\|^2 \\right) \\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Step 7: Bounding the double sum\n\nNote that each index \\(m\\) appears in at most \\(C\\) sets \\(S_n\\) (since each \\(f_m\\) is orthogonal to at most \\(C\\) others).  \nThus:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_n \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} |a_m|^2 \\le C \\sum_m |a_m|^2,\n\\]\nand similarly  \n\\[\n\\sum_n \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} \\|f_m\\|^2 \\le C \\sum_m \\|f_m\\|^2.\n\\]\n\nBut we have a product of two sums inside the second factor. We can bound:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_n \\left( \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} |a_m|^2 \\right)\n\\left( \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} \\|f_m\\|^2 \\right)\n\\le \\left( \\sup_n \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} \\|f_m\\|^2 \\right)\n\\sum_n \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} |a_m|^2.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\sup_n \\sum_{m \\in S_n, m \\neq n} \\|f_m\\|^2\\) is not necessarily bounded — the norms can be large. So this direct approach fails unless we have more structure.\n\n---\n\n### Step 8: A better approach — using the orthogonality directly\n\nWe can use the fact that the sequence has bounded "overlap" in the sense of a **graph with bounded degree**.  \nDefine a graph where vertices are indices \\(n\\), and edges connect \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) if \\(\\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle \\neq 0\\).  \nEach vertex has degree at most \\(C\\).\n\nWe want to bound  \n\\[\n\\left\\| \\sum_n a_n f_n \\right\\|^2 = \\sum_{n,m} a_n \\overline{a_m} \\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle.\n\\]\n\nWe can write this as  \n\\[\n\\sum_n |a_n|^2 \\|f_n\\|^2 + \\sum_{\\text{edges } (n,m)} (a_n \\overline{a_m} \\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle + \\overline{a_n} a_m \\overline{\\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle}).\n\\]\n\nFor each edge,  \n\\[\n|a_n \\overline{a_m} \\langle f_n, f_m \\rangle| \\le |a_n| |a_m| \\|f_n\\| \\|f_m\\|.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\\[\n\\left| \\sum_{\\text{edges}} \\cdots \\right| \\le 2 \\sum_{\\text{edges}} |a_n| |a_m| \\|f_n\\| \\|f_m\\|.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Step 9: Applying the weighted Cauchy–Schwarz on the graph\n\nLet \\(b_n = |a_n| \\|f_n\\|\\). Then the cross term is bounded by  \n\\[\n2 \\sum_{\\text{edges}} b_n b_m.\n\\]\n\nSince the graph has maximum degree \\(C\\), we can bound  \n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{edges}} b_n b_m \\le \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_n \\sum_{m \\in S_n} (b_n^2 + b_m^2)\n\\]\nby AM–GM.\n\nFor each \\(n\\), there are at most \\(C\\) neighbors, so  \n\\[\n\\sum_n \\sum_{m \\in S_n} b_n^2 \\le C \\sum_n b_n^2,\n\\]\nand similarly  \n\\[\n\\sum_n \\sum_{m \\in S_n} b_m^2 \\le C \\sum_m b_m^2.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{edges}} b_n b_m \\le \\frac{1}{2} (C \\sum_n b_n^2 + C \\sum_m b_m^2) = C \\sum_n b_n^2.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Step 10: Final bound\n\nTherefore  \n\\[\n\\left\\| \\sum_n a_n f_n \\right\\|^2 \\le \\sum_n b_n^2 + 2 C \\sum_n b_n^2 = (1 + 2C) \\sum_n b_n^2.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(b_n^2 = |a_n|^2 \\|f_n\\|^2\\), so  \n\\[\n\\left\\| \\sum_n a_n f_n \\right\\|^2 \\le (1+2C) \\sum_n |a_n|^2 \\|f_n\\|^2.\n\\]\n\nTaking \\(a_n = 1\\) for all \\(n\\) (or more precisely, truncating to finite sums and then taking limits), we get  \n\\[\n\\left\\| \\sum_n f_n \\right\\|^2 \\le (1+2C) \\sum_n \\|f_n\\|^2.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\\[\n\\left\\| \\sum_n f_n \\right\\| \\le \\sqrt{1+2C} \\left( \\sum_n \\|f_n\\|^2 \\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Step 11: Conclusion\n\nWe have shown that the desired inequality holds with  \n\\[\nC\' = \\sqrt{1+2C},\n\\]\nwhich is an absolute constant depending only on \\(C\\).  \n\nTherefore, the answer is **yes**, such an absolute constant \\(C\'\\) exists.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Yes, with } C\' = \\sqrt{1+2C}.}\n\\]'}, 'events': [], 'links': [], 'resource': {'attributes': {'service.name': 'agentops', 'agentops.project.id': 'dummy', 'imported_libraries': ['string', 'sympy', 'uuid', 'json', 'asyncio']}, 'schema_url': ''}}, {'name': 'openai.chat.completion', 'context': {'trace_id': '0xcd25701358303365c33bcc4395b49d45', 'span_id': '0x630bf50bb0170332', 'trace_state': '[]'}, 'kind': 'SpanKind.CLIENT', 'parent_id': '0x5aab183f0c2dd78f', 'start_time': '2026-08-25T15:18:18.643758Z', 'end_time': '2026-08-25T15:18:21.217472Z', 'status': {'status_code': 'OK'}, 'attributes': {'gen_ai.request.type': 'chat', 'gen_ai.system': 'OpenAI', 'gen_ai.request.model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'gen_ai.request.max_tokens': 4000, 'gen_ai.request.temperature': 0, 'gen_ai.request.top_p': 0.99, 'gen_ai.request.streaming': False, 'gen_ai.prompt.0.role': 'system', 'gen_ai.prompt.0.content': 'You are a helpful assistant.', 'gen_ai.prompt.1.role': 'user', 'gen_ai.prompt.1.content': '\nTask: Evaluate if the current memory is complete and accurate enough to answer the query, or if more tools are needed.\n\nContext:\n- **Query:** Consider an infinite sequence of smooth, compactly supported functions $\\{f_n\\}_{n \\geq 1}$ on $\\mathbb{R}$ such that each $f_n$ is orthogonal to $C$ other functions in the sequence, where $C$ is an absolute constant. Determine if there exists an absolute constant $C\'$ such that the following inequality holds:\n\\[\\left\\|\\sum_{n \\geq 1}f_{n}\\right\\|_{L^{2}(\\mathbb{R})} \\leq C\'\\left(\\sum_{n \\geq 1}\\|f_{n}\\|_{L^{2}(\\mathbb{R})}^{2}\\right)^{1/2}\\] When ready, output the final answer enclosed in <answer> and </answer> tags. Do not generate any content after the </answer> tag.\n- **Available Tools:** [\'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\']\n- **Toolbox Metadata:** {\'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\': {\'tool_name\': \'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\', \'tool_description\': \'A generalized tool that takes query from the user, and answers the question step by step to the best of its ability. It can also accept an image.\', \'tool_version\': \'1.0.0\', \'input_types\': {\'query\': \'str - The query that includes query from the user to guide the agent to generate response.\'}, \'output_type\': \'str - The generated response to the original query\', \'demo_commands\': [{\'command\': \'execution = tool.execute(query="Summarize the following text in a few lines")\', \'description\': \'Generate a short summary given the query from the user.\'}], \'user_metadata\': {\'limitation\': \'\\nThe Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool may provide hallucinated or incorrect responses.\\n\', \'best_practice\': "\\nFor optimal results with the Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool:\\n1. Use it for general queries or tasks that don\'t require specialized knowledge or specific tools in the toolbox.\\n2. Provide clear, specific query.   \\n3. Use it to answer the original query through step by step reasoning for tasks without complex or multi-step reasoning.\\n4. For complex queries, break them down into subtasks and use the tool multiple times.\\n5. Use it as a starting point for complex tasks, then refine with specialized tools.\\n6. Verify important information from its responses.\\n"}, \'require_llm_engine\': True}}\n- **Initial Analysis:** <answer>\n**Summary of Query:**  \nThe query asks whether an infinite sequence of smooth, compactly supported functions on ℝ, with each function orthogonal to at most C other functions (C an absolute constant), satisfies a global L²-norm inequality of the form ‖Σ fₙ‖ ≤ C′ (Σ ‖fₙ‖²)^{1/2} for some absolute constant C′. This is a functional analysis / harmonic analysis problem about almost-orthogonality and square-function estimates.\n\n**Necessary Skills:**  \n1. **Functional Analysis (L² spaces, orthogonality, Bessel’s inequality)** – To understand the structure of orthogonal systems and how near-orthogonality affects norm bounds.  \n2. **Harmonic Analysis (almost-orthogonality, Cotlar–Stein lemma, Littlewood–Paley theory)** – The condition “each fₙ orthogonal to at most C others” is a classic almost-orthogonality setup, often handled via Cotlar–Stein or Schur test.  \n3. **Inequalities and Operator Theory** – To bound the norm of the sum operator via matrix estimates (e.g., Schur’s test, Young’s inequality).  \n4. **Mathematical Rigor and Proof Construction** – To verify whether the inequality holds for all such sequences, possibly by constructing a counterexample if false.\n\n**Necessary Tools:**  \n- **Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool** – This is the only available tool. It can be used to generate a step-by-step reasoning process, propose a proof or counterexample, and check the validity of the inequality. Since the query is mathematical and self-contained, the tool can attempt to apply known results (e.g., Cotlar–Stein lemma) to derive the bound. However, the tool may hallucinate, so its output must be critically reviewed.\n\n**How the Tool Helps:**  \n- It can break down the problem: first, note that the condition implies a sparse interaction matrix (each row has at most C non-zero entries). Then, using Schur’s test or the Cotlar–Stein lemma, one can show that the operator T: (aₙ) → Σ aₙ fₙ is bounded from ℓ² to L² with norm ≤ C′ (where C′ depends only on C). This directly yields the desired inequality. The tool can generate this reasoning and provide the final answer.\n\n**Additional Considerations:**  \n- The smoothness and compact support are not essential; the result holds for any L² functions with the same orthogonality condition.  \n- The absolute constant C′ will depend on C (e.g., C′ = √C or C+1), but not on the sequence.  \n- The tool’s response should be verified: the Cotlar–Stein lemma requires a stronger condition (almost orthogonality with decay), but here the sparse orthogonality condition is sufficient via a simple Schur test on the Gram matrix.  \n- If the tool fails to produce a rigorous proof, one could manually apply the Schur test: define the Gram matrix G with Gᵢⱼ = ⟨fᵢ, fⱼ⟩. Each row has at most C non-zero entries, and |Gᵢⱼ| ≤ ‖fᵢ‖‖fⱼ‖. Then for any finite sum, ‖Σ aₙ fₙ‖² = Σᵢⱼ aᵢ aⱼ Gᵢⱼ ≤ Σᵢ |aᵢ|² Σⱼ |Gᵢⱼ| ≤ C Σᵢ |aᵢ|² ‖fᵢ‖² (using Cauchy–Schwarz and the sparsity). This gives the bound with C′ = √C.  \n- The final answer is **yes**, such a constant C′ exists (e.g., C′ = √C).\n</answer>\n- **Memory (Tools Used & Results):** {\'Action Step 1\': {\'tool_name\': \'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\', \'sub_goal\': \'Provide a detailed step-by-step proof or verification using functional analysis techniques and tools available, specifically focusing on verifying the result obtained from our initial approach.\\n\\n4.\', \'command\': \'execution = tool.execute(query="Consider an infinite sequence of smooth, compactly supported functions {f_n}_{n ≥ 1} on R such that each f_n is orthogonal to C other functions in the sequence, where C is an absolute constant. Determine if there exists an absolute constant C\\\' such that the following inequality holds: ||sum_{n ≥ 1} f_n||_{L^2(R)} ≤ C\\\' (sum_{n ≥ 1} ||f_n||_{L^2(R)}^2)^{1/2}. Provide a detailed step-by-step proof or verification using functional analysis techniques, specifically focusing on verifying the result obtained from our initial approach using the Schur test.")\', \'result\': [\'We are given an infinite sequence of smooth, compactly supported functions \\\\(\\\\{f_n\\\\}_{n \\\\ge 1}\\\\) on \\\\(\\\\mathbb{R}\\\\), with the property that each \\\\(f_n\\\\) is orthogonal to all but at most \\\\(C\\\\) other functions in the sequence, where \\\\(C\\\\) is an absolute constant.  \\nWe want to determine whether there exists an absolute constant \\\\(C\\\'\\\\) such that  \\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\| \\\\sum_{n \\\\ge 1} f_n \\\\right\\\\|_{L^2(\\\\mathbb{R})} \\\\le C\\\' \\\\left( \\\\sum_{n \\\\ge 1} \\\\|f_n\\\\|_{L^2(\\\\mathbb{R})}^2 \\\\right)^{1/2}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 1: Reformulating the problem\\n\\nLet \\\\(H = L^2(\\\\mathbb{R})\\\\). Define the operator \\\\(T: \\\\ell^2 \\\\to H\\\\) by  \\n\\n\\\\[\\nT(a) = \\\\sum_{n=1}^\\\\infty a_n f_n,\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(a = (a_n) \\\\in \\\\ell^2\\\\).  \\nWe want to show that \\\\(T\\\\) is bounded, i.e., \\\\(\\\\|T\\\\|_{\\\\text{op}} \\\\le C\\\'\\\\) for some absolute constant \\\\(C\\\'\\\\).\\n\\nThe norm of \\\\(T\\\\) is given by  \\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|T\\\\|_{\\\\text{op}}^2 = \\\\sup_{\\\\|a\\\\|_{\\\\ell^2} \\\\le 1} \\\\left\\\\| \\\\sum_{n} a_n f_n \\\\right\\\\|_H^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nExpanding the square:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\| \\\\sum_n a_n f_n \\\\right\\\\|_H^2 \\n= \\\\sum_{n,m} a_n \\\\overline{a_m} \\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 2: Using the orthogonality structure\\n\\nWe know each \\\\(f_n\\\\) is orthogonal to all but at most \\\\(C\\\\) other functions. That means for each fixed \\\\(n\\\\), the set  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\{ m : \\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle \\\\neq 0 \\\\}\\n\\\\]  \\nhas size at most \\\\(C+1\\\\) (including \\\\(n\\\\) itself).  \\nThus the Gram matrix \\\\(G = (\\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle)\\\\) has at most \\\\(C+1\\\\) nonzero entries per row and per column.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 3: Applying the Schur test\\n\\nWe want to bound the quadratic form  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{n,m} a_n \\\\overline{a_m} G_{n,m}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBy Cauchy–Schwarz:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left| \\\\sum_{n,m} a_n \\\\overline{a_m} G_{n,m} \\\\right|\\n\\\\le \\\\sum_{n,m} |a_n| |a_m| |G_{n,m}|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWe can apply the **Schur test** for nonnegative matrices. Let \\\\(M_{n,m} = |G_{n,m}|\\\\).  \\nWe need to find positive weights \\\\(w_n > 0\\\\) such that  \\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_m M_{n,m} w_m \\\\le \\\\lambda w_n \\\\quad \\\\text{for all } n,\\n\\\\]\\nand  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_n M_{n,m} w_n \\\\le \\\\lambda w_m \\\\quad \\\\text{for all } m.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTake \\\\(w_n = 1\\\\) for all \\\\(n\\\\). Then:\\n\\n- For each \\\\(n\\\\),  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_m M_{n,m} = \\\\sum_{m: \\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle \\\\neq 0} |\\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle|.\\n\\\\]  \\nBy Cauchy–Schwarz,  \\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle| \\\\le \\\\|f_n\\\\| \\\\|f_m\\\\|.\\n\\\\]  \\nBut we don’t have uniform bounds on \\\\(\\\\|f_n\\\\|\\\\) — they can be arbitrary. So we need a different approach.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 4: Using the structure more carefully\\n\\nWe can write  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\| \\\\sum_n a_n f_n \\\\right\\\\|^2 \\n= \\\\sum_n |a_n|^2 \\\\|f_n\\\\|^2 + \\\\sum_{n \\\\neq m} a_n \\\\overline{a_m} \\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe cross terms only occur when \\\\(\\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle \\\\neq 0\\\\), and for each \\\\(n\\\\) there are at most \\\\(C\\\\) such \\\\(m \\\\neq n\\\\).\\n\\nThus:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left| \\\\sum_{n \\\\neq m} a_n \\\\overline{a_m} \\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle \\\\right|\\n\\\\le \\\\sum_{n} \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} |a_n| |a_m| |\\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle|,\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(|S_n| \\\\le C\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 5: Applying Cauchy–Schwarz to the cross terms\\n\\nFor each \\\\(n\\\\),  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} |a_m| |\\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle|\\n\\\\le \\\\left( \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} |a_m|^2 \\\\right)^{1/2}\\n\\\\left( \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} |\\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle|^2 \\\\right)^{1/2}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nNow,  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} |\\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle|^2\\n\\\\le \\\\|f_n\\\\|^2 \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} \\\\|f_m\\\\|^2\\n\\\\]\\nby Cauchy–Schwarz on each inner product.\\n\\nSo  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} |a_m| |\\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle|\\n\\\\le \\\\|f_n\\\\| \\\\left( \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} |a_m|^2 \\\\right)^{1/2}\\n\\\\left( \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} \\\\|f_m\\\\|^2 \\\\right)^{1/2}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 6: Summing over \\\\(n\\\\)\\n\\nWe have  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{n} \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} |a_n| |a_m| |\\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle|\\n\\\\le \\\\sum_n |a_n| \\\\|f_n\\\\| \\\\left( \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} |a_m|^2 \\\\right)^{1/2}\\n\\\\left( \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} \\\\|f_m\\\\|^2 \\\\right)^{1/2}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nApply Cauchy–Schwarz again:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\le \\\\left( \\\\sum_n |a_n|^2 \\\\|f_n\\\\|^2 \\\\right)^{1/2}\\n\\\\left( \\\\sum_n \\\\left( \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} |a_m|^2 \\\\right)\\n\\\\left( \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} \\\\|f_m\\\\|^2 \\\\right) \\\\right)^{1/2}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 7: Bounding the double sum\\n\\nNote that each index \\\\(m\\\\) appears in at most \\\\(C\\\\) sets \\\\(S_n\\\\) (since each \\\\(f_m\\\\) is orthogonal to at most \\\\(C\\\\) others).  \\nThus:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_n \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} |a_m|^2 \\\\le C \\\\sum_m |a_m|^2,\\n\\\\]\\nand similarly  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_n \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} \\\\|f_m\\\\|^2 \\\\le C \\\\sum_m \\\\|f_m\\\\|^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBut we have a product of two sums inside the second factor. We can bound:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_n \\\\left( \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} |a_m|^2 \\\\right)\\n\\\\left( \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} \\\\|f_m\\\\|^2 \\\\right)\\n\\\\le \\\\left( \\\\sup_n \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} \\\\|f_m\\\\|^2 \\\\right)\\n\\\\sum_n \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} |a_m|^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBut \\\\(\\\\sup_n \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n, m \\\\neq n} \\\\|f_m\\\\|^2\\\\) is not necessarily bounded — the norms can be large. So this direct approach fails unless we have more structure.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 8: A better approach — using the orthogonality directly\\n\\nWe can use the fact that the sequence has bounded "overlap" in the sense of a **graph with bounded degree**.  \\nDefine a graph where vertices are indices \\\\(n\\\\), and edges connect \\\\(n\\\\) and \\\\(m\\\\) if \\\\(\\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle \\\\neq 0\\\\).  \\nEach vertex has degree at most \\\\(C\\\\).\\n\\nWe want to bound  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\| \\\\sum_n a_n f_n \\\\right\\\\|^2 = \\\\sum_{n,m} a_n \\\\overline{a_m} \\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWe can write this as  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_n |a_n|^2 \\\\|f_n\\\\|^2 + \\\\sum_{\\\\text{edges } (n,m)} (a_n \\\\overline{a_m} \\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle + \\\\overline{a_n} a_m \\\\overline{\\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle}).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor each edge,  \\n\\\\[\\n|a_n \\\\overline{a_m} \\\\langle f_n, f_m \\\\rangle| \\\\le |a_n| |a_m| \\\\|f_n\\\\| \\\\|f_m\\\\|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left| \\\\sum_{\\\\text{edges}} \\\\cdots \\\\right| \\\\le 2 \\\\sum_{\\\\text{edges}} |a_n| |a_m| \\\\|f_n\\\\| \\\\|f_m\\\\|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 9: Applying the weighted Cauchy–Schwarz on the graph\\n\\nLet \\\\(b_n = |a_n| \\\\|f_n\\\\|\\\\). Then the cross term is bounded by  \\n\\\\[\\n2 \\\\sum_{\\\\text{edges}} b_n b_m.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince the graph has maximum degree \\\\(C\\\\), we can bound  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{\\\\text{edges}} b_n b_m \\\\le \\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\sum_n \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n} (b_n^2 + b_m^2)\\n\\\\]\\nby AM–GM.\\n\\nFor each \\\\(n\\\\), there are at most \\\\(C\\\\) neighbors, so  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_n \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n} b_n^2 \\\\le C \\\\sum_n b_n^2,\\n\\\\]\\nand similarly  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_n \\\\sum_{m \\\\in S_n} b_m^2 \\\\le C \\\\sum_m b_m^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{\\\\text{edges}} b_n b_m \\\\le \\\\frac{1}{2} (C \\\\sum_n b_n^2 + C \\\\sum_m b_m^2) = C \\\\sum_n b_n^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 10: Final bound\\n\\nTherefore  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\| \\\\sum_n a_n f_n \\\\right\\\\|^2 \\\\le \\\\sum_n b_n^2 + 2 C \\\\sum_n b_n^2 = (1 + 2C) \\\\sum_n b_n^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBut \\\\(b_n^2 = |a_n|^2 \\\\|f_n\\\\|^2\\\\), so  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\| \\\\sum_n a_n f_n \\\\right\\\\|^2 \\\\le (1+2C) \\\\sum_n |a_n|^2 \\\\|f_n\\\\|^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTaking \\\\(a_n = 1\\\\) for all \\\\(n\\\\) (or more precisely, truncating to finite sums and then taking limits), we get  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\| \\\\sum_n f_n \\\\right\\\\|^2 \\\\le (1+2C) \\\\sum_n \\\\|f_n\\\\|^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus  \\n\\\\[\