in| = 1/4\\\\) or \\\\(3/4\\\\), and maximum 1 at \\\\(|\\\\sin| = 0, 1/2, 1\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 7 – Second equation: \\\\(x = 4 g(f(\\\\cos(3\\\\pi y)))\\\\)\\n\\nBy symmetry, same structure but with \\\\(\\\\cos(3\\\\pi y)\\\\) instead of \\\\(\\\\sin(2\\\\pi x)\\\\), and variables swapped.\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n- For \\\\(|\\\\cos(3\\\\pi y)| \\\\in [\\\\frac14, \\\\frac12]\\\\):  \\n  \\\\(x = 4|\\\\cos(3\\\\pi y)| - 1\\\\).\\n\\n- For \\\\(|\\\\cos(3\\\\pi y)| \\\\in [\\\\frac12, \\\\frac34]\\\\):  \\n  \\\\(x = 3 - 4|\\\\cos(3\\\\pi y)|\\\\).\\n\\n- For \\\\(|\\\\cos(3\\\\pi y)| < \\\\frac14\\\\):  \\n  \\\\(x = 1 - 4|\\\\cos(3\\\\pi y)|\\\\).\\n\\n- For \\\\(|\\\\cos(3\\\\pi y)| > \\\\frac34\\\\):  \\n  \\\\(x = 4|\\\\cos(3\\\\pi y)| - 3\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 8 – Intersection conditions\\n\\nWe need both:\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny = F(x) \\\\quad\\\\text{and}\\\\quad x = G(y)\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(F\\\\) and \\\\(G\\\\) are the piecewise functions above.\\n\\nBecause both functions are periodic and symmetric, we can analyze in one period.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 9 – Periodicity\\n\\n- \\\\(\\\\sin(2\\\\pi x)\\\\) has period 1 in \\\\(x\\\\).  \\n- \\\\(\\\\cos(3\\\\pi y)\\\\) has period \\\\(2/3\\\\) in \\\\(y\\\\) (since \\\\(\\\\cos(3\\\\pi(y+2/3)) = \\\\cos(3\\\\pi y + 2\\\\pi)\\\\)).\\n\\nSo the system is periodic in \\\\(x\\\\) with period 1 and in \\\\(y\\\\) with period \\\\(2/3\\\\).\\n\\nWe can restrict to \\\\(x \\\\in [0,1]\\\\), \\\\(y \\\\in [0, 2/3]\\\\) and count intersections, then multiply by the number of periods in the plane? But the problem likely expects finite count in the whole plane? Actually, since both are periodic, there will be infinitely many intersections unless we restrict domain. The problem likely means in one fundamental rectangle \\\\([0,1] \\\\times [0, 2/3]\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 10 – Symmetry\\n\\nBoth \\\\(F\\\\) and \\\\(G\\\\) are even in their arguments:  \\n\\\\(F(x)\\\\) depends on \\\\(|\\\\sin(2\\\\pi x)|\\\\), s