ny content after the </answer> tag. When ready, output the final answer enclosed in <answer> and </answer> tags. Do not generate any content after the </answer> tag.\n- **Sub-Goal:** Determine and provide an exact count of all possible ways to place chips on a 5×5 grid while adhering to all given conditions.\n\n4.\n- **Tool Name:** Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\n- **Tool Metadata:** {\'tool_name\': \'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\', \'tool_description\': \'A generalized tool that takes query from the user, and answers the question step by step to the best of its ability. It can also accept an image.\', \'tool_version\': \'1.0.0\', \'input_types\': {\'query\': \'str - The query that includes query from the user to guide the agent to generate response.\'}, \'output_type\': \'str - The generated response to the original query\', \'demo_commands\': [{\'command\': \'execution = tool.execute(query="Summarize the following text in a few lines")\', \'description\': \'Generate a short summary given the query from the user.\'}], \'user_metadata\': {\'limitation\': \'\\nThe Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool may provide hallucinated or incorrect responses.\\n\', \'best_practice\': "\\nFor optimal results with the Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool:\\n1. Use it for general queries or tasks that don\'t require specialized knowledge or specific tools in the toolbox.\\n2. Provide clear, specific query.   \\n3. Use it to answer the original query through step by step reasoning for tasks without complex or multi-step reasoning.\\n4. For complex queries, break them down into subtasks and use the tool multiple times.\\n5. Use it as a starting point for complex tasks, then refine with specialized tools.\\n6. Verify important information from its responses.\\n"}, \'require_llm_engine\': True}\n- **Relevant Data:** No additional context is needed beyond what is provided in the query analysis.\n\n3.\n\nInstructions:\n1.  Analyze the tool\'s required parameters from its metadata.\n2.  Construct valid Python code that addresses the sub-goal using the provided context and data.\n3.  The command must include at least one call to `tool.execute()`.\n4.  Each `tool.execute()` call must be assigned to a variable named **`execution`**.\n5.  Please give the exact numbers and parameters should be used in the `tool.execute()` call.\n\nOutput Format:\nPresent your response in the following structured format. Do not include any extra text or explanations.\n\nGenerated Command:\n```python\n<command>\n```\n\nExample1:\nGenerated Command:\n```python\nexecution = tool.execute(query="Summarize the following porblom:"Isaac has 100 toys, masa gets ...., how much are their together?")\n```\n\nExample2:\nGenerated Command:\n```python\nexecution = tool.execute(query=["Methanol", "function of hyperbola", "Fermat\'s Last Theorem"])\n```\n', 'gen_ai.response.id': '0f7d52e8-fa06-4f0c-aa64-afeee0e0af16', 'gen_ai.response.model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'gen_ai.openai.system_fingerprint': 'a26a7955944dc5c60445bff77fac9c8e', 'gen_ai.usage.total_tokens': 906, 'gen_ai.usage.prompt_tokens': 779, 'gen_ai.usage.completion_tokens': 127, 'gen_ai.completion.0.finish_reason': 'stop', 'gen_ai.completion.0.role': 'assistant', 'gen_ai.completion.0.content': 'Generated Command:\n```python\nexecution = tool.execute(query="There is a collection of 25 indistinguishable white chips and 25 indistinguishable black chips. Find the number of ways to place some of these chips in the 25 unit cells of a 5x5 grid such that: each cell contains at most one chip; all chips in the same row and all chips in the same column have the same colour; any additional chip placed on the grid would violate one or more of the previous two conditions. Provide the exact count of all possible ways to place chips on a 5x5 grid while adhering to all given conditions.")\n```'}, 'events': [], 'links': [], 'resource': {'attributes': {'service.name': 'agentops', 'agentops.project.id': 'dummy', 'imported_libraries': ['string', 'sympy', 'uuid', 'json', 'asyncio']}, 'schema_url': ''}}, {'name': 'openai.chat.completion', 'context': {'trace_id': '0x85f65679ec756c4c3986c9f96c9de2c1', 'span_id': '0x8d6cccc434ac9d06', 'trace_state': '[]'}, 'kind': 'SpanKind.CLIENT', 'parent_id': None, 'start_time': '2026-08-25T15:36:24.618448Z', 'end_time': '2026-08-25T15:36:38.724110Z', 'status': {'status_code': 'OK'}, 'attributes': {'gen_ai.request.type': 'chat', 'gen_ai.system': 'OpenAI', 'gen_ai.request.model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'gen_ai.request.max_tokens': 4000, 'gen_ai.request.temperature': 0, 'gen_ai.request.top_p': 0.99, 'gen_ai.request.streaming': False, 'gen_ai.prompt.0.role': 'system', 'gen_ai.prompt.0.content': 'You are a helpful assistant.', 'gen_ai.prompt.1.role': 'user', 'gen_ai.prompt.1.content': 'There is a collection of 25 indistinguishable white chips and 25 indistinguishable black chips. Find the number of ways to place some of these chips in the 25 unit cells of a 5x5 grid such that: each cell contains at most one chip; all chips in the same row and all chips in the same column have the same colour; any additional chip placed on the grid would violate one or more of the previous two conditions. Provide the exact count of all possible ways to place chips on a 5x5 grid while adhering to all given conditions.', 'gen_ai.response.id': '377d2f40-5fb1-4c6f-be26-9b680170b7a4', 'gen_ai.response.model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'gen_ai.openai.system_fingerprint': 'a26a7955944dc5c60445bff77fac9c8e', 'gen_ai.usage.total_tokens': 1920, 'gen_ai.usage.prompt_tokens': 123, 'gen_ai.usage.completion_tokens': 1797, 'gen_ai.completion.0.finish_reason': 'stop', 'gen_ai.completion.0.role': 'assistant', 'gen_ai.completion.0.content': 'We need to carefully parse the problem.\n\nWe have a 5×5 grid (25 cells). We have 25 white and 25 black chips, all indistinguishable within their color. We place some chips (possibly zero in some cells) such that:\n\n1. Each cell has at most one chip.\n2. In each row, all chips are the same color.\n3. In each column, all chips are the same color.\n4. The placement is **maximal** in the sense that adding any additional chip (in any empty cell) would violate condition 2 or 3.\n\nWe need to count the number of such maximal configurations.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1 – Understanding the constraints\n\nLet’s label rows \\( R_1, \\dots, R_5 \\) and columns \\( C_1, \\dots, C_5 \\).\n\nCondition 2: In a given row, all chips are the same color.  \nCondition 3: In a given column, all chips are the same color.\n\nThis means: If a row has both a white and a black chip, that’s forbidden. Similarly for columns.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2 – Structure of a valid placement\n\nConsider a cell \\((i,j)\\). If it has a chip of color \\(c\\), then:\n\n- Row \\(i\\) must be entirely color \\(c\\) (if it has any chips).\n- Column \\(j\\) must be entirely color \\(c\\) (if it has any chips).\n\nThus, if a row has any chips, it has a single color assigned to it. Similarly for columns.\n\nSo we can think of assigning to each row a color (or "empty" if no chips in that row), and to each column a color (or "empty"). Then a cell gets a chip if and only if the row color and column color match and are not empty.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3 – Maximality condition\n\nIf a row is empty (no chips), then we could add a chip in any column that is empty or matches that row’s color? Wait, careful:\n\nIf a row is empty, it has no assigned color. If we try to add a chip in that row in some column, that column might have a color. Then the new chip would be that column’s color, but then the row would have chips of that color only (since it’s the first chip in that row), so condition 2 is okay. But condition 3: the column already has that color, so it’s okay. So we could add a chip. Therefore, for maximality, **no row can be completely empty** unless all columns are also empty? Let’s check:\n\nIf a row is empty, we can put a chip in any cell of that row, as long as the column is not empty? Actually if the column is empty too, then we can put a chip of either color? But then the row would have that color, and the column would have that color, so it’s fine. So indeed, if any row is empty, we can add a chip somewhere. So maximality implies **every row must have at least one chip**. Similarly, every column must have at least one chip.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4 – Consequence\n\nIf every row has at least one chip, then each row has a color (since all chips in that row are same color). Similarly, each column has a color.\n\nNow, consider a cell \\((i,j)\\). If row \\(i\\) has color \\(r_i\\) and column \\(j\\) has color \\(c_j\\), then:\n\n- If \\(r_i = c_j\\), we must place a chip of that color in that cell (otherwise we could add one and it wouldn’t violate anything).\n- If \\(r_i \\neq c_j\\), we cannot place a chip there (because it would violate either row or column color consistency).\n\nThus the placement is completely determined by the row colors and column colors.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5 – Counting possible color assignments\n\nWe have 5 rows, each assigned either White (W) or Black (B).  \nWe have 5 columns, each assigned W or B.\n\nBut we must ensure that **every row has at least one chip** and **every column has at least one chip**.\n\nA row gets a chip if there is at least one column with the same color as that row.  \nA column gets a chip if there is at least one row with the same color as that column.\n\n---\n\n### Step 6 – Conditions for non-empty rows/columns\n\nLet \\(w_r\\) = number of white rows, \\(b_r = 5 - w_r\\) = number of black rows.  \nLet \\(w_c\\) = number of white columns, \\(b_c = 5 - w_c\\) = number of black columns.\n\n- A white row gets a chip iff \\(w_c \\ge 1\\).  \n- A black row gets a chip iff \\(b_c \\ge 1\\).  \n- A white column gets a chip iff \\(w_r \\ge 1\\).  \n- A black column gets a chip iff \\(b_r \\ge 1\\).\n\nFor maximality, we need:\n\n- Every white row must have at least one white column → \\(w_c \\ge 1\\) if \\(w_r > 0\\).\n- Every black row must have at least one black column → \\(b_c \\ge 1\\) if \\(b_r > 0\\).\n- Every white column must have at least one white row → \\(w_r \\ge 1\\) if \\(w_c > 0\\).\n- Every black column must have at least one black row → \\(b_r \\ge 1\\) if \\(b_c > 0\\).\n\n---\n\n### Step 7 – Possible cases\n\nWe have \\(w_r\\) from 0 to 5, \\(w_c\\) from 0 to 5.\n\nCase 1: \\(w_r = 0\\) (all rows black). Then we need \\(b_c \\ge 1\\) for black rows to have chips. Also, if \\(w_c > 0\\), then white columns need \\(w_r \\ge 1\\), which fails. So \\(w_c\\) must be 0. So only possibility: \\(w_r=0, w_c=0\\). That gives all rows black, all columns black → all cells black. That’s valid and maximal? Check: every row has chips (all 5 cells), every column has chips. Yes. So 1 configuration.\n\nCase 2: \\(w_r = 5\\) (all rows white). Symmetric: only \\(w_c=5\\) works. 1 configuration.\n\nCase 3: \\(1 \\le w_r \\le 4\\). Then we need:\n\n- \\(w_c \\ge 1\\) (for white rows)\n- \\(b_c \\ge 1\\) (for black rows) → \\(5 - w_c \\ge 1\\) → \\(w_c \\le 4\\)\n- Also \\(w_r \\ge 1\\) (already true) and \\(b_r \\ge 1\\) (already true since \\(w_r \\le 4\\)).\n\nSo the only restriction is \\(1 \\le w_c \\le 4\\).\n\nThus for each \\(w_r\\) from 1 to 4, \\(w_c\\) can be 1,2,3,4.\n\n---\n\n### Step 8 – Counting\n\nNumber of ways to choose which rows are white: \\(\\binom{5}{w_r}\\).  \nNumber of ways to choose which columns are white: \\(\\binom{5}{w_c}\\).\n\nSo total count:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{w_r=1}^{4} \\sum_{w_c=1}^{4} \\binom{5}{w_r} \\binom{5}{w_c}\n\\]\n\nThis is:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\sum_{w_r=1}^{4} \\binom{5}{w_r} \\right) \\cdot \\left( \\sum_{w_c=1}^{4} \\binom{5}{w_c} \\right)\n\\]\n\nWe know \\(\\sum_{k=0}^5 \\binom{5}{k} = 2^5 = 32\\).  \nSo \\(\\sum_{k=1}^4 \\binom{5}{k} = 32 - \\binom{5}{0} - \\binom{5}{5} = 32 - 1 - 1 = 30\\).\n\nThus the product is \\(30 \\times 30 = 900\\).\n\n---\n\n### Step 9 – Add the two monochrome cases\n\nWe had 1 all-black and 1 all-white configuration. So total:\n\n\\[\n900 + 2 = 902\n\\]\n\n---\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{902}\n\\]'}, 'events': [], 'links': [], 'resource': {'attributes': {'service.name': 'agentops', 'agentops.project.id': 'dummy', 'imported_libraries': ['string', 'sympy', 'uuid', 'json', 'asyncio']}, 'schema_url': ''}}, {'name': 'openai.chat.completion', 'context': {'trace_id': '0xcd25701358303365c33bcc4395b49d45', 'span_id': '0xf9f90ac2852782eb', 'trace_state': '[]'}, 'kind': 'SpanKind.CLIENT', 'parent_id': '0x5aab183f0c2dd78f', 'start_time': '2026-08-25T15:36:38.726252Z', 'end_time': '2026-08-25T15:36:41.214604Z', 'status': {'status_code': 'OK'}, 'attributes': {'gen_ai.request.type': 'chat', 'gen_ai.system': 'OpenAI', 'gen_ai.request.model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'gen_ai.request.max_tokens': 4000, 'gen_ai.request.temperature': 0, 'gen_ai.request.top_p': 0.99, 'gen_ai.request.streaming': False, 'gen_ai.prompt.0.role': 'system', 'gen_ai.prompt.0.content': 'You are a helpful assistant.', 'gen_ai.prompt.1.role': 'user', 'gen_ai.prompt.1.content': '\nTask: Evaluate if the current memory is complete and accurate enough to answer the query, or if more tools are needed.\n\nContext:\n- **Query:** There is a collection of $25$ indistinguishable white chips and $25$ indistinguishable black chips. Find the number of ways to place some of these chips in the $25$ unit cells of a $5\\times5$ grid such that:\n\neach cell contains at most one chip\nall chips in the same row and all chips in the same column have the same colour\nany additional chip placed on the grid would violate one or more of the previous two conditions.\n\nWhen ready, output the final answer enclosed in <answer> and </answer> tags. Do not generate any content after the </answer> tag. When ready, output the final answer enclosed in <answer> and </answer> tags. Do not generate any content after the </answer> tag.\n- **Available Tools:** [\'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\']\n- **Toolbox Metadata:** {\'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\': {\'tool_name\': \'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\', \'tool_description\': \'A generalized tool that takes query from the user, and answers the question step by step to the best of its ability. It can also accept an image.\', \'tool_version\': \'1.0.0\', \'input_types\': {\'query\': \'str - The query that includes query from the user to guide the agent to generate response.\'}, \'output_type\': \'str - The generated response to the original query\', \'demo_commands\': [{\'command\': \'execution = tool.execute(query="Summarize the following text in a few lines")\', \'description\': \'Generate a short summary given the query from the user.\'}], \'user_metadata\': {\'limitation\': \'\\nThe Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool may provide hallucinated or incorrect responses.\\n\', \'best_practice\': "\\nFor optimal results with the Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool:\\n1. Use it for general queries or tasks that don\'t require specialized knowledge or specific tools in the toolbox.\\n2. Provide clear, specific query.   \\n3. Use it to answer the original query through step by step reasoning for tasks without complex or multi-step reasoning.\\n4. For complex queries, break them down into subtasks and use the tool multiple times.\\n5. Use it as a starting point for complex tasks, then refine with specialized tools.\\n6. Verify important information from its responses.\\n"}, \'require_llm_engine\': True}}\n- **Initial Analysis:** <answer>\n**Summary of Query:**  \nThe query asks for the number of ways to place indistinguishable white and black chips on a 5×5 grid (25 cells) such that: (1) each cell has at most one chip, (2) all chips in each row and each column are the same color, and (3) the placement is maximal—adding any additional chip would break condition (1) or (2). The answer must be a single integer.\n\n**Necessary Skills:**  \n1. **Combinatorial reasoning** – To count valid configurations under row/column color constraints and maximality.  \n2. **Logical deduction** – To derive the structure of maximal placements (e.g., each row and column must be either all white, all black, or empty, with no empty row/column unless fully filled).  \n3. **Case analysis** – To enumerate possible patterns of row/column color assignments and count distinct chip placements.\n\n**Necessary Tools:**  \n- **Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool** – This tool can provide a step-by-step solution to the query. It is suitable because the problem is a self-contained combinatorial counting task that does not require external data or specialized software. The tool can break down the reasoning into cases (e.g., number of white rows, black rows, empty rows, etc.) and compute the final count.\n\n**How the Tool Helps:**  \n- It can systematically analyze the constraints: each row and column must have a uniform color (or be empty), and maximality forces every row and column to be non-empty (otherwise a chip could be added).  \n- It can enumerate all possible assignments of row colors (white, black, empty) and column colors, then count the number of valid chip placements that satisfy both row and column constraints.  \n- It can handle the combinatorial counting (e.g., using inclusion-exclusion or direct enumeration) and produce the final integer.\n\n**Additional Considerations:**  \n- The problem involves indistinguishable chips, so only the pattern of occupied cells and their colors matters, not which specific chip is which.  \n- Maximality implies that no empty cell can be filled without violating uniformity—this forces every row and column to have at least one chip, and also forces that if a row is white, all columns intersecting it must be white (or empty), and vice versa.  \n- The final answer is a specific integer; the tool should be verified for correctness, as it may hallucinate. A manual check or cross-referencing with known results is advisable.\n</answer>\n- **Memory (Tools Used & Results):** {\'Action Step 1\': {\'tool_name\': \'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\', \'sub_goal\': \'Determine and provide an exact count of all possible ways to place chips on a 5×5 grid while adhering to all given conditions.\\n\\n4.\', \'command\': \'execution = tool.execute(query="There is a collection of 25 indistinguishable white chips and 25 indistinguishable black chips. Find the number of ways to place some of these chips in the 25 unit cells of a 5x5 grid such that: each cell contains at most one chip; all chips in the same row and all chips in the same column have the same colour; any additional chip placed on the grid would violate one or more of the previous two conditions. Provide the exact count of all possible ways to place chips on a 5x5 grid while adhering to all given conditions.")\', \'result\': [\'We need to carefully parse the problem.\\n\\nWe have a 5×5 grid (25 cells). We have 25 white and 25 black chips, all indistinguishable within their color. We place some chips (possibly zero in some cells) such that:\\n\\n1. Each cell has at most one chip.\\n2. In each row, all chips are the same color.\\n3. In each column, all chips are the same color.\\n4. The placement is **maximal** in the sense that adding any additional chip (in any empty cell) would violate condition 2 or 3.\\n\\nWe need to count the number of such maximal configurations.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 1 – Understanding the constraints\\n\\nLet’s label rows \\\\( R_1, \\\\dots, R_5 \\\\) and columns \\\\( C_1, \\\\dots, C_5 \\\\).\\n\\nCondition 2: In a given row, all chips are the same color.  \\nCondition 3: In a given column, all chips are the same color.\\n\\nThis means: If a row has both a white and a black chip, that’s forbidden. Similarly for columns.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 2 – Structure of a valid placement\\n\\nConsider a cell \\\\((i,j)\\\\). If it has a chip of color \\\\(c\\\\), then:\\n\\n- Row \\\\(i\\\\) must be entirely color \\\\(c\\\\) (if it has any chips).\\n- Column \\\\(j\\\\) must be entirely color \\\\(c\\\\) (if it has any chips).\\n\\nThus, if a row has any chips, it has a single color assigned to it. Similarly for columns.\\n\\nSo we can think of assigning to each row a color (or "empty" if no chips in that row), and to each column a color (or "empty"). Then a cell gets a chip if and only if the row color and column color match and are not empty.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 3 – Maximality condition\\n\\nIf a row is empty (no chips), then we could add a chip in any column that is empty or matches that row’s color? Wait, careful:\\n\\nIf a row is empty, it has no assigned color. If we try to add a chip in that row in some column, that column might have a color. Then the new chip would be that column’s color, but then the row would have chips of that color only (since it’s the first chip in that row), so condition 2 is okay. But condition 3: the column already has that color, so it’s okay. So we could add a chip. Therefore, for maximality, **no row can be completely empty** unless all columns are also empty? Let’s check:\\n\\nIf a row is empty, we can put a chip in any cell of that row, as long as the column is not empty? Actually if the column is empty too, then we can put a chip of either color? But then the row would have that color, and the column would have that color, so it’s fine. So indeed, if any row is empty, we can add a chip somewhere. So maximality implies **every row must have at least one chip**. Similarly, every column must have at least one chip.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 4 – Consequence\\n\\nIf every row has at least one chip, then each row has a color (since all chips in that row are same color). Similarly, each column has a color.\\n\\nNow, consider a cell \\\\((i,j)\\\\). If row \\\\(i\\\\) has color \\\\(r_i\\\\) and column \\\\(j\\\\) has color \\\\(c_j\\\\), then:\\n\\n- If \\\\(r_i = c_j\\\\), we must place a chip of that color in that cell (otherwise we could add one and it wouldn’t violate anything).\\n- If \\\\(r_i \\\\neq c_j\\\\), we cannot place a chip there (because it would violate either row or column color consistency).\\n\\nThus the placement is completely determined by the row colors and column colors.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 5 – Counting possible color assignments\\n\\nWe have 5 rows, each assigned either White (W) or Black (B).  \\nWe have 5 columns, each assigned W or B.\\n\\nBut we must ensure that **every row has at least one chip** and **every column has at least one chip**.\\n\\nA row gets a chip if there is at least one column with the same color as that row.  \\nA column gets a chip if there is at least one row with the same color as that column.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 6 – Conditions for non-empty rows/columns\\n\\nLet \\\\(w_r\\\\) = number of white rows, \\\\(b_r = 5 - w_r\\\\) = number of black rows.  \\nLet \\\\(w_c\\\\) = number of white columns, \\\\(b_c = 5 - w_c\\\\) = number of black columns.\\n\\n- A white row gets a chip iff \\\\(w_c \\\\ge 1\\\\).  \\n- A black row gets a chip iff \\\\(b_c \\\\ge 1\\\\).  \\n- A white column gets a chip iff \\\\(w_r \\\\ge 1\\\\).  \\n- A black column gets a chip iff \\\\(b_r \\\\ge 1\\\\).\\n\\nFor maximality, we need:\\n\\n- Every white row must have at least one white column → \\\\(w_c \\\\ge 1\\\\) if \\\\(w_r > 0\\\\).\\n- Every black row must have at least one black column → \\\\(b_c \\\\ge 1\\\\) if \\\\(b_r > 0\\\\).\\n- Every white column must have at least one white row → \\\\(w_r \\\\ge 1\\\\) if \\\\(w_c > 0\\\\).\\n- Every black column must have at least one black row → \\\\(b_r \\\\ge 1\\\\) if \\\\(b_c > 0\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 7 – Possible cases\\n\\nWe have \\\\(w_r\\\\) from 0 to 5, \\\\(w_c\\\\) from 0 to 5.\\n\\nCase 1: \\\\(w_r = 0\\\\) (all rows black). Then we need \\\\(b_c \\\\ge 1\\\\) for black rows to have chips. Also, if \\\\(w_c > 0\\\\), then white columns need \\\\(w_r \\\\ge 1\\\\), which fails. So \\\\(w_c\\\\) must be 0. So only possibility: \\\\(w_r=0, w_c=0\\\\). That gives all rows black, all columns black → all cells black. That’s valid and maximal? Check: every row has chips (all 5 cells), every column has chips. Yes. So 1 configuration.\\n\\nCase 2: \\\\(w_r = 5\\\\) (all rows white). Symmetric: only \\\\(w_c=5\\\\) works. 1 configuration.\\n\\nCase 3: \\\\(1 \\\\le w_r \\\\le 4\\\\). Then we need:\\n\\n- \\\\(w_c \\\\ge 1\\\\) (for white rows)\\n- \\\\(b_c \\\\ge 1\\\\) (for black rows) → \\\\(5 - w_c \\\\ge 1\\\\) → \\\\(w_c \\\\le 4\\\\)\\n- Also \\\\(w_r \\\\ge 1\\\\) (already true) and \\\\(b_r \\\\ge 1\\\\) (already true since \\\\(w_r \\\\le 4\\\\)).\\n\\nSo the only restriction is \\\\(1 \\\\le w_c \\\\le 4\\\\).\\n\\nThus for each \\\\(w_r\\\\) from 1 to 4, \\\\(w_c\\\\) can be 1,2,3,4.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 8 – Counting\\n\\nNumber of ways to choose which rows are white: \\\\(\\\\binom{5}{w_r}\\\\).  \\nNumber of ways to choose which columns are white: \\\\(\\\\binom{5}{w_c}\\\\).\\n\\nSo total count:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{w_r=1}^{4} \\\\sum_{w_c=1}^{4} \\\\binom{5}{w_r} \\\\binom{5}{w_c}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left( \\\\sum_{w_r=1}^{4} \\\\binom{5}{w_r} \\\\right) \\\\cdot \\\\left( \\\\sum_{w_c=1}^{4} \\\\binom{5}{w_c} \\\\right)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWe know \\\\(\\\\sum_{k=0}^5 \\\\binom{5}{k} = 2^5 = 32\\\\).  \\nSo \\\\(\\\\sum_{k=1}^4 \\\\binom{5}{k} = 32 - \\\\binom{5}{0} - \\\\binom{5}{5} = 32 - 1 - 1 = 30\\\\).\\n\\nThus the product is \\\\(30 \\\\times 30 = 900\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 9 – Add the two monochrome cases\\n\\nWe had 1 all-black and 1 all-white configuration. So total:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n900 + 2 = 902\\n\\\\]\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{902}\\n\\\\]\']}}\n\nInstructions:\n1.  Review the query, initial analysis, and memory.\n2.  Assess the completeness of the memory: Does it fully address all parts of the query?\n3.  Check for potential issues:\n    -   Are there any inconsistencies or contradictions?\n    -   Is any information ambiguous or in need of verification?\n4.  Determine if any unused tools could provide missing information.\n\nFinal Determina