lors.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 5 – Counting possible color assignments\\n\\nWe have 5 rows, each assigned either White (W) or Black (B).  \\nWe have 5 columns, each assigned W or B.\\n\\nBut we must ensure that **every row has at least one chip** and **every column has at least one chip**.\\n\\nA row gets a chip if there is at least one column with the same color as that row.  \\nA column gets a chip if there is at least one row with the same color as that column.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 6 – Conditions for non-empty rows/columns\\n\\nLet \\\\(w_r\\\\) = number of white rows, \\\\(b_r = 5 - w_r\\\\) = number of black rows.  \\nLet \\\\(w_c\\\\) = number of white columns, \\\\(b_c = 5 - w_c\\\\) = number of black columns.\\n\\n- A white row gets a chip iff \\\\(w_c \\\\ge 1\\\\).  \\n- A black row gets a chip iff \\\\(b_c \\\\ge 1\\\\).  \\n- A white column gets a chip iff \\\\(w_r \\\\ge 1\\\\).  \\n- A black column gets a chip iff \\\\(b_r \\\\ge 1\\\\).\\n\\nFor maximality, we need:\\n\\n- Every white row must have at least one white column → \\\\(w_c \\\\ge 1\\\\) if \\\\(w_r > 0\\\\).\\n- Every black row must have at least one black column → \\\\(b_c \\\\ge 1\\\\) if \\\\(b_r > 0\\\\).\\n- Every white column must have at least one white row → \\\\(w_r \\\\ge 1\\\\) if \\\\(w_c > 0\\\\).\\n- Every black column must have at least one black row → \\\\(b_r \\\\ge 1\\\\) if \\\\(b_c > 0\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 7 – Possible cases\\n\\nWe have \\\\(w_r\\\\) from 0 to 5, \\\\(w_c\\\\) from 0 to 5.\\n\\nCase 1: \\\\(w_r = 0\\\\) (all rows black). Then we need \\\\(b_c \\\\ge 1\\\\) for black rows to have chips. Also, if \\\\(w_c > 0\\\\), then white columns need \\\\(w_r \\\\ge 1\\\\), which fails. So \\\\(w_c\\\\) must be 0. So only possibility: \\\\(w_r=0, w_c=0\\\\). That gives all rows black, all columns black → all cells black. That’s valid and maximal? Check: every row has chips (all 5 cells), every column has chips. Yes. So 1 configuration.\\n\\nCase 2: \\\\(w_r 