(3c - 3) \\equiv 0 \\)  \n\\( 6(1-a) + 3(c-1) \\equiv 0 \\)  \nDivide by 3: \\( 2(1-a) + (c-1) \\equiv 0 \\)  \nSo \\( c - 1 \\equiv 2(a-1) \\pmod{7} \\)  \nThus \\( c \\equiv 2a - 1 \\pmod{7} \\).\n\nFrom Condition 1 and 4:  \n(1) \\( 6 + 2b + 3c + d \\equiv 0 \\)  \n(4) \\( 6a + 2b + 3c + 1 \\equiv 0 \\)  \nSubtract: \\( (6 - 6a) + (d - 1) \\equiv 0 \\)  \nSo \\( d - 1 \\equiv 6(a-1) \\pmod{7} \\)  \nThus \\( d \\equiv 6a - 5 \\pmod{7} \\).\n\nNow we have:  \n\\( b \\equiv 3a - 2 \\pmod{7} \\)  \n\\( c \\equiv 2a - 1 \\pmod{7} \\)  \n\\( d \\equiv 6a - 5 \\pmod{7} \\).\n\nDigits must be between 0 and 9, and \\( a \\) between 1 and 9.\n\nWe want the largest \\( N \\), so start with largest \\( a = 9 \\):\n\nFor \\( a=9 \\):  \n\\( b \\equiv 27 - 2 = 25 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7} \\) → \\( b=4 \\) or \\( 11 \\) (invalid), so \\( b=4 \\).  \n\\( c \\equiv 18 - 1 = 17 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7} \\) → \\( c=3 \\) or \\( 10 \\) (invalid), so \\( c=3 \\).  \n\\( d \\equiv 54 - 5 = 49 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7} \\) → \\( d=0 \\) or \\( 7 \\). For largest, take \\( d=7 \\).\n\nSo \\( N = 9437 \\). Check conditions:\n\n- Change thousands to 1: 1437 ÷ 7 = 205.285... Wait, check: 7×205=1435, remainder 2. Not divisible. So fail.\n\nSo \\( d=7 \\) fails? Let's check:  \nFor \\( a=9, b=4, c=3, d=7 \\):  \nCondition 1: \\( 6 + 2(4) + 3(3) + 7 = 6+8+9+7=30 \\), 30 mod 7 = 2, not 0. So indeed fails.  \nSo maybe \\( d=0 \\):  \n\\( 6+8+9+0=23 \\), mod 7 = 2, still fails. So \\( a=9 \\) fails.\n\nTry \\( a=8 \\):  \n\\( b \\equiv 24-2=22 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\) → \\( b=1 \\) or 8.  \n\\( c \\equiv 16-1=15 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\) → \\( c=1 \\) or 8.  \n\\( d \\equiv 48-5=43 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\) → \\( d=1 \\) or 8.\n\nLargest: \\( b=8, c=8, d=8 \\) gives \\( N=8888 \\).  \nCheck condition 1: \\( 6+16+24+8=54 \\), mod 7 = 5, fail.  \nTry \\( b=8, c=8, d=1 \\): sum = 6+16+24+1=47, mod 7 = 5, fail.  \nTry \\( b=8, c=1, d=8 \\): sum=6+16+3+8=33, mod 7=5, fail.  \nTry \\( b=1, c=8, d=8 \\): sum=6+2+24+8=40, 