reatest four-digit integer \\(N\\) such that changing any one of its digits to 1 yields a number divisible by 7. Then compute \\(Q+R\\), where \\(Q\\) and \\(R\\) are the quotient and remainder when \\(N\\) is divided by 1000.\n\n**Necessary Skills:**  \n1. **Number theory** – Divisibility rules, modular arithmetic (mod 7), and properties of digit manipulation.  \n2. **Systematic enumeration/logical deduction** – Efficiently testing four-digit numbers under constraints without brute-forcing all 9000 possibilities.  \n3. **Basic arithmetic** – Division, quotient/remainder computation.\n\n**Necessary Tools:**  \n- **Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool** – Can be used to perform step-by-step reasoning, test candidate numbers, and verify divisibility conditions. It can handle the modular arithmetic and systematic search required.\n\n**How the tool helps:**  \n- It can break the problem into subtasks: express the condition for each digit position (units, tens, hundreds, thousands) as a congruence modulo 7, then solve for \\(N\\) systematically.  \n- It can compute \\(N \\div 1000\\) to find \\(Q\\) and \\(R\\), and sum them.\n\n**Additional Considerations:**  \n- The tool may hallucinate; verify the final answer by manual checks or cross-referencing with modular arithmetic.  \n- The problem requires careful handling of the "greatest" condition – ensure the solution is maximal.  \n- The answer should be a single integer \\(Q+R\\).\n</answer>\n- **Actions Taken:** {\'Action Step 1\': {\'tool_name\': \'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\', \'sub_goal\': \'Determine if changing each digit of \\\\(N\\\\) (a four-digit number) to 1 makes the resulting number divisible by 7.\\n\\n4.\', \'command\': \'execution = tool.execute(query="Let N be the greatest four-digit positive integer with the property that whenever one of its digits is changed to 1, the resulting number is divisible by 7. Let Q and R be the quotient and remainder, respectively, when N is divided by 1000. Find Q+R.")\', \'result\': ["We need the greatest four-digit number \\\\( N \\\\) such that changing any single digit to 1 gives a number divisible by 7.\\n\\nLet \\\\( N = 1000a + 100b + 10c + d \\\\), where \\\\( a \\\\in \\\\{1,\\\\dots,9\\\\} \\\\), \\\\( b,c,d \\\\in \\\\{0,\\\\dots,9\\\\} \\\\).\\n\\nThe condition:  \\n- Change thousands digit to 1: \\\\( 1000 + 100b + 10c + d \\\\equiv 0 \\\\pmod{7} \\\\)  \\n- Change hundreds digit to 1: \\\\( 1000a + 100 + 10c + d \\\\equiv 0 \\\\pmod{7} \\\\)  \\n- Change tens digit to 1: \\\\( 1000a + 100b + 10 + d \\\\equiv 0 \\\\pmod{7} \\\\)  \\n- Change units digit to 1: \\\\( 1000a + 100b + 10c + 1 \\\\equiv 0 \\\\pmod{7} \\\\)\\n\\nFirst, note \\\\( 1000 \\\\equiv 6 \\\\pmod{7} \\\\), \\\\( 100 \\\\equiv 2 \\\\pmod{7} \\\\), \\\\( 10 \\\\equiv 3 \\\\pmod{7} \\\\).\\n\\nSo \\\\( N \\\\equiv 6a + 2b + 3c + d \\\\pmod{7} \\\\).\\n\\n**Condition 1:** Thousands digit → 1:  \\n\\\\( 6(1) + 2b + 3c + d \\\\equiv 0 \\\\pmod{7} \\\\)  \\nSo \\\\( 6 + 2b + 3c + d \\\\equiv 0 \\\\).\\n\\n**Condition 2:** Hundreds digit → 1:  \\n\\\\( 6a + 2(1) + 3c + d \\\\equiv 0 \\\\)  \\nSo \\\\( 6a + 2 + 3c + d \\\\equiv 0 \\\\).\\n\\n**Condition 3:** Tens digit → 1:  \\n\\\\( 6a + 2b + 3(1) + d \\\\equiv 0 \\\\)  \\nSo \\\\( 6a + 2b + 3 + d \\\\equiv 0 \\\\).\\n\\n**Condition 4:** Units digit → 1:  \\n\\\\( 6a + 2b + 3c + 1 \\\\equiv 0 \\\\).\\n\\nNow subtract equations to find relations.\\n\\nFrom Condition 1 and 2:  \\n(1) \\\\( 6 + 2b + 3c + d \\\\equiv 0 \\\\)  \\n(2) \\\\( 6a + 2 + 3c + d \\\\equiv 0 \\\\)  \\nSubtract: \\\\( (6 - 6a) + (2b - 2) \\\\equiv 0 \\\\)  \\n\\\\( 6(1-a) + 2(b-1) \\\\equiv 0 \\\\)  \\nDivide by 2: \\\\( 3(1-a) + (b-1) \\\\equiv 0 \\\\)  \\nSo \\\\( b - 1 \\\\equiv 3(a-1) \\\\pmod{7} \\\\)  \\nThus \\\\( b \\\\equiv 3a - 2 \\\\pmod{7} \\\\).\\n\\nFrom Condition 1 and 3:  \\n(1) \\\\( 6 + 2b + 3c + d \\\\equiv 0 \\\\)  \\n(3) \\\\( 6a + 2b + 3 + d \\\\equiv 0 \\\\)  \\nSubtract: \\\\( (6 - 6a) + (3c - 3) \\\\equiv 0 \\\